 {"id":"SETTLE-1","title":"P2: verify the 15-frame step settle across terrain \u2014 1 frame of margin, and a mid-step sample silently inverts state_changed","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"Found by playing the game through the new bridge rather than reading the loop. Pressing DOWN (ROM: walkable) with an 8-frame wait returned state_changed=False and the player did not appear to move; the NEXT press (UP, ROM: blocked) then reported state_changed=True with the tile moving y+1, i.e. DOWNWARD. That is the mid-step sample: the step started by the previous press completes during the following press's wait, so each press's state_changed is read against the wrong frame and can invert a blocked direction into a move. Re-running with a poll-until-is_moving()-is-false settle measured the walk completing in 14 frames, while the genuinely blocked UP settled in 2 (no walk ever started). So the code under test is NOT at fault: cron_runner.py:720-721 sets PRESS_FRAMES=5 + STEP_FORWARD=15 with the comment 'settle without triggering D-pad key repeat', applied in the main loop at :5036-5037, and 15 frames covers a 14-frame walk. My bridge's 8-frame wait was the bug and is fixed (scripts/ dropped to poll is_moving() with a 40x2 frame cap, settle reported back in the response). THE RESIDUAL RISK, which is why this row exists: 15 is exactly 1 frame above the measured settle on Pallet Town grass. Any longer transition animation \u2014 a ledge hop, stairs, a door warp, surf \u2014 could exceed 15 frames, and the failure mode is silent and plausible: the loop reads a real move as a failed one (or vice versa) and its blocked-direction logic goes wrong. That is a candidate contributor to the direction-locking DIST-1 logged at 41% of cycles and to the 8 episodes spent inside Red's House 1F. Done-when: settle time is measured on at least a ledge, a stairway and a door transition; STEP_FORWARD is either confirmed with real margin on each, or replaced by polling is_moving() so the wait adapts to the animation instead of assuming it.","created_at":"2026-09-27T22:06:16.943492+00:00"}
 {"id":"PLAY-1","title":"P2: keep the session-scoped live-play bridge \u2014 it is how a human and the agent can inspect the model's real input together","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"New surface, added because reviewing the model's judgement from outside its inputs was guesswork. scripts/game_bridge.py boots one emulator and serves the SAME text the decision model receives \u2014 RAMReader.observe() (src/core/ram_reader.py:1604) rendered by state_projection.build() (src/core/state_projection.py:102) with the same DEFAULT_MECHANICS and the same cross-cycle material \u2014 over loopback, and applies button presses. scripts/play.py is the client. VERIFIED LIVE: observe returns the identical 1233-character projection (GAME/MAP/PLAYER TILE/PARTY/SCREEN/TOPOLOGY from ROM collision truth/ADJACENT TERRAIN/LOCAL COLLISION MAP/VISIBLE EXITS/TILES VISITED/RECENT EVENTS/GOAL/MECHANICS); press moves the player and reports before/after; save and load work; frame capture writes a PNG. Cross-checked against the pixels: the captured frame shows a large building directly north of the player, which is exactly why the ROM reports UP=blocked \u2014 text and image agree. ACCESS MODEL, and it must stay this way: off by default; NOT a Hermes plugin and nothing autostarts it, so no other session inherits it; binds 127.0.0.1 only; refuses to boot without a token file; refuses a token file that is group- or world-readable; every request must carry the token and is compared with secrets.compare_digest. Verified the negative: a request with no token returns {\"ok\": false, \"error\": \"unauthorized\"}. Enabling is per session \u2014 mint ~/.hermes/aipp_bridge/session.token (0600), start the bridge, done; revoke by killing the process and deleting the token. Done-when: the enable/revoke steps are written into the repo docs, and the bridge is referenced from the agentic-harness spec as the operator's input-side inspection tool. Explicitly NOT done-when-it-would-be-tempting: this must never become a public or globally-available tool surface.","created_at":"2026-09-27T22:06:16.943492+00:00"}
 {"id":"REV-1","title":"cron_runner has no no-key/stub path \u2014 fresh-clone first run crashes with unhandled ValueError","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":3,"depends_on":[],"blocks":[],"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"cron_runner.py (the README sole recommended entry point) has no no-key or stub path. A fresh clone with no OPENROUTER_API_KEY crashes: ValueError OpenRouter API key not found, raised at src/core/ai_client.py line 548 from cron_runner.py line 3984, exit 1, AFTER printing Booting from checkpoint and Starting run banners. README lines 448-455 and .env.example both promise stub mode when no key is set, but that stub path exists only in the legacy src/game_loop.py path, not cron_runner. Verified live: clone at 1668362, no .env, python3 cron_runner.py --cycles 3 -> crash. With a present-but-invalid key the run degrades gracefully (teacher 401 falls back to JEV action), so the gap is specifically the absent-key path.","created_at":"2026-09-27T22:25:13.899717+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-27T22:25:13.899717+00:00","detail":{"fingerprint":"2307d7a060d51d0a998f9ada8d1a24682a4eb8d9155da266dffe208c1966099c"}}
